# Toolbar

Commands, controls, and search at the top of a window, placed by what they do.

Also called: NSToolbar, toolbar item, ToolbarItem, ToolbarItemGroup, placement, principal, primaryAction, customize toolbar, toolbar(id:), ToolbarSpacer.

## Contents

- Use when
- Built from
- Rules
- Pitfalls
- Key APIs
- Variants
- Related

## Use when

- Add `.toolbar { }` to a view in a window, and its items go in the window's toolbar.
- Give each `ToolbarItem` a placement that says what it's for, like `.navigation` or `.principal`, and SwiftUI puts it where that kind of item belongs on the Mac.
- Use `.toolbar(id:)` with an ID on each item to let people customize the toolbar: they Control-click it and choose Customize Toolbar to add, remove, and rearrange items.

## Built from

.toolbar
├── ToolbarItem(placement: .navigation)
├── ToolbarItem(placement: .principal)
├── ToolbarSpacer(.flexible)
└── ToolbarItemGroup
    └── Button…

## Rules

- **Use symbols, not words.** Simple, familiar symbols are quicker to scan and take less space. Text works better only for actions a symbol can't show, like Edit.
- **Title the window after its content, not your app.** A title like the mailbox or document name tells people where they are. The app's name tells them nothing about what's in the window.
- **Put every toolbar command in the menu bar too.** People can hide or customize the toolbar, so it can't be the only home for a command.
- **Keep it to a few groups.** Group items by what they do, and aim for no more than three groups. Too many makes the toolbar feel cluttered.

## Pitfalls

- **Narrow windows move items into an overflow menu.** Choose a few items that matter, so little ends up hidden there.
- **Keep window titles short.** The HIG suggests under 15 characters, so the title leaves room for the toolbar's items.

## Key APIs

- `.toolbar { }`: Adds items to the window's toolbar.
- `ToolbarItem(placement:)`: One item, placed by its purpose.
- `.navigation`: Leading edge, before the title: back and forward.
- `.principal`: The center of the toolbar.
- `.primaryAction`: The command people use most here.
- `.toolbar(id:)`: A toolbar people can customize.
- `ToolbarSpacer(.fixed)`: Space between items or groups (macOS 26).
- `.navigationSubtitle(_:)`: A line of context with the title.

## Variants

### Usage

```swift
List(["Lunch on Friday?", "Your receipt"], id: \.self) { subject in
    Text(subject)
}
.navigationTitle("Inbox")
.toolbar {
    Button("New Message", systemImage: "square.and.pencil") { }
}
```

### Placements

`.navigation` items go at the leading edge before the title, and `.principal` items sit in the center. Mark the command people use most `.primaryAction`.

```swift
@State private var filter = "All"

List(["Lunch on Friday?", "Your receipt"], id: \.self) { Text($0) }
    .navigationTitle("Inbox")
    .navigationSubtitle("12 messages")
    .toolbar {
        ToolbarItem(placement: .navigation) {
            Button("Back", systemImage: "chevron.left") { }
        }
        ToolbarItem(placement: .principal) {
            Picker("Filter", selection: $filter) {
                Text("All").tag("All")
                Text("Unread").tag("Unread")
            }
            .pickerStyle(.segmented)
        }
        ToolbarItem(placement: .primaryAction) {
            Button("New Message", systemImage: "square.and.pencil") { }
        }
    }
```

**Note:** A real `.toolbar` in a page would land in this app's own toolbar, so the preview draws one. The Placements window above shows the real thing.

### A toolbar people can customize

Give `.toolbar(id:)` and every `ToolbarItem` an ID. Control-click the toolbar in the window to customize it.

```swift
@State private var isBold = false
@State private var isItalic = false

TextEditor(text: .constant("Hello"))
    .toolbar(id: "editor") {
        ToolbarItem(id: "bold") {
            Toggle("Bold", systemImage: "bold", isOn: $isBold)
        }
        ToolbarItem(id: "italic") {
            Toggle("Italic", systemImage: "italic", isOn: $isItalic)
        }
        ToolbarSpacer(.fixed)
        ToolbarItem(id: "share") {
            ShareLink(item: "Hello")
        }
    }
```

**Note:** Add `ToolbarCommands()` to your scene's `.commands` to put a Customize Toolbar item in the View menu too.

### Space between groups

`ToolbarSpacer(.fixed)` adds a standard gap, and `.flexible` pushes items apart. Keep text-labeled buttons apart so they don't read as one.

```swift
List(["Notes"], id: \.self) { Text($0) }
    .toolbar {
        ToolbarItem {
            Button("Share", systemImage: "square.and.arrow.up") { }
        }
        ToolbarSpacer(.fixed)
        ToolbarItem {
            Button("Delete", systemImage: "trash") { }
        }
    }
```

