# Observable

One model class that every window, sidebar, and inspector reads and updates.

Also called: @Observable, @Bindable, Observation, model, view model, store, ObservableObject, @StateObject, @EnvironmentObject, shared state.

## Contents

- Pitfalls
- Guide
- Key APIs
- Variants
- Related

## Pitfalls

- **Views update only for what their `body` reads.** A view that shows a title redraws when the title changes, not when some other property of the same model does.
- **A missing model is a crash.** Reading `@Environment(Model.self)` when no view above put one there stops the app. Declare the property as `Model?` where it may be missing.
- **Replaces `ObservableObject` for new code.** No `@Published`, `@StateObject`, or `@EnvironmentObject`: use `@State`, `@Bindable`, and `@Environment(Model.self)`.

## Guide

A Mac app often shows the same data in several places at once: two windows, a sidebar and an inspector, the Settings window. Put that data in a class marked `@Observable`, create it once, and hand it to each place that needs it.

SwiftUI watches which properties each view's `body` reads. When one of them changes, those views redraw, in every window. There's nothing to publish or subscribe to.

The window below and a real window share one model. Open the window, tick off an article there, and watch the count here change.

### Which property wrapper

- The view that creates the model: `@State private var library = Library()`
- A view that shows it: A plain property: `var library: Library`
- A view whose controls edit it: `@Bindable var library: Library`, then `$library.name`
- A view far below, or in another scene: `@Environment(Library.self) private var library`

## Key APIs

- `@Observable class Model`: Makes every stored property observable.
- `@State private var model = Model()`: Owns the model; create it once, at the top.
- `.environment(model)`: Shares it with a scene or view and everything inside.
- `@Environment(Model.self)`: Reads the shared model anywhere below.
- `@Bindable var model`: Makes `$model.title` bindings for controls.
- `@ObservationIgnored`: Leaves a property out of tracking.

## Variants

### Create it once, share it with every scene

Own the model with `@State` in your `App`, then pass it to each scene with `.environment(_:)`. Every window and Settings get the same instance.

```swift
@Observable
class ReadingList {
    var articles: [Article] = []
    var showsUnreadCount = true

    var unreadCount: Int {
        articles.filter { !$0.isRead }.count
    }
}

struct Article: Identifiable {
    let id = UUID()
    var title: String
    var isRead = false
}

@main
struct ReaderApp: App {
    @State private var readingList = ReadingList()

    var body: some Scene {
        WindowGroup {
            ContentView()
        }
        .environment(readingList)

        Settings {
            SettingsView()
        }
        .environment(readingList)
    }
}
```

**Note:** Change the setting in the small window and the toolbar of the other one follows. They read the same model.

### Edit it with @Bindable

Declare the view's model `@Bindable` to get bindings like `$article.title` for controls, such as the fields in an inspector.

```swift
@Observable
class EditableArticle {
    var title = "Designing for the Mac"
    var author = "Sam Lee"
    var isRead = false
}

struct ArticleInspector: View {
    @Bindable var article: EditableArticle

    var body: some View {
        Form {
            TextField("Title", text: $article.title)
            TextField("Author", text: $article.author)
            Toggle("Read", isOn: $article.isRead)
        }
    }
}
```

### Read it from the environment

Deep in the view tree, read the model with `@Environment(ReadingList.self)`. For bindings, wrap it in a local `@Bindable` inside `body`.

```swift
struct UnreadSettings: View {
    @Environment(ReadingList.self) private var readingList

    var body: some View {
        @Bindable var readingList = readingList
        Form {
            Toggle("Show unread count", isOn: $readingList.showsUnreadCount)
            LabeledContent("Unread", value: readingList.unreadCount, format: .number)
        }
    }
}
```

### Leave a property out with @ObservationIgnored

Mark caches and other bookkeeping `@ObservationIgnored`. Changing them never redraws a view.

```swift
@Observable
class PhotoLibrary {
    var photos: [URL] = []

    @ObservationIgnored
    var thumbnailCache: [URL: Data] = [:]
}
```

