# Environment

Values that flow down from a window to every view inside it, from control size to actions.

Also called: @Environment, EnvironmentValues, @Entry, EnvironmentKey, context, React context, useContext, dependency injection, controlSize, openWindow.

## Contents

- Pitfalls
- Guide
- Key APIs
- Variants
- Related

## Pitfalls

- **It only flows down.** A value you set applies to that view and the views inside it. Parents and siblings never see it.
- **Prefer the dedicated modifier.** Write `.controlSize(.small)` rather than `.environment(\.controlSize, .small)`, and give your own values a modifier too.
- **Actions live here too.** `openWindow`, `dismissWindow`, and `openSettings` are values you call like functions.

## Guide

The environment is a set of values every view can read without anyone passing them in: the window's color scheme, the size of its controls, whether it's the window people are working in, and actions like opening another window.

Set a value on a container and every view inside gets it, like CSS inheritance or React context. A view reads it with `@Environment`.

One `.controlSize` on the container sets the size of every control inside it.

### Values you'll use on the Mac

- controlSize: Mini, small, regular, or large controls. Set it with `.controlSize(_:)`.
- openWindow: Opens a `Window` or a `WindowGroup` by its ID. An open `Window` comes to the front.
- openSettings: Opens your app's Settings window, the same one ⌘, opens.
- appearsActive: Whether views should look active. False in a window that isn't the key window.
- dismissWindow: Closes a window by its ID.
- colorScheme: Light or dark, for the rare view that has to draw itself differently.

## Key APIs

`@Environment(\.controlSize)`, `.environment(\.key, value)`, `@Entry var name = default`, `\.openWindow`, `\.openSettings`, `\.appearsActive`, `\.dismissWindow`

## Variants

### Open another window

Read `openWindow` and call it with the ID of a `Window` or `WindowGroup` in your app. This one opens a real window.

```swift
@main
struct ReaderApp: App {
    var body: some Scene {
        WindowGroup {
            ContentView()
        }
        Window("Activity", id: "activity") {
            ActivityView()
        }
    }
}

struct ShowActivityButton: View {
    @Environment(\.openWindow) private var openWindow

    var body: some View {
        Button("Show Activity") {
            openWindow(id: "activity")
        }
    }
}
```

**Note:** A `Window` also gets an item in the Window menu, so people can reopen it without your button.

### Open Settings from your own button

Call `openSettings` to show your Settings window, for example from an empty state. This one opens this app's Settings.

```swift
struct AccountsEmptyState: View {
    @Environment(\.openSettings) private var openSettings

    var body: some View {
        ContentUnavailableView {
            Label("No Accounts", systemImage: "person.crop.circle")
        } actions: {
            Button("Add an Account…") {
                openSettings()
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Look inactive when the window isn't key

Read `appearsActive` in custom views and drop the accent color when it's false. Click another window to see this badge turn gray.

```swift
struct LiveBadge: View {
    @Environment(\.appearsActive) private var appearsActive

    var body: some View {
        Label("Live", systemImage: "dot.radiowaves.left.and.right")
            .font(.callout.weight(.semibold))
            .padding(.horizontal, 10)
            .padding(.vertical, 4)
            .foregroundStyle(appearsActive ? .white : .secondary)
            .background(appearsActive ? Color.accentColor : Color.secondary.opacity(0.2), in: .capsule)
    }
}
```

**Note:** Selection in lists and tables turns gray on its own. Views and styles you draw yourself need to check `appearsActive`.

### Your own value with @Entry

Declare a value with `@Entry` in an `EnvironmentValues` extension, and add a modifier that sets it. Rows read it; one modifier changes them all.

```swift
enum RowDensity {
    case compact, comfortable
}

extension EnvironmentValues {
    @Entry var rowDensity: RowDensity = .comfortable
}

extension View {
    func rowDensity(_ density: RowDensity) -> some View {
        environment(\.rowDensity, density)
    }
}

struct EpisodeRow: View {
    @Environment(\.rowDensity) private var density
    let title: String

    var body: some View {
        Label(title, systemImage: "waveform")
            .padding(.vertical, density == .compact ? 2 : 8)
    }
}

VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 0) {
    EpisodeRow(title: "Episode 12")
    EpisodeRow(title: "Episode 11")
}
.rowDensity(.compact)
```

